  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1015 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nThe main challenge for the forecast this week is pinpointing\nexactly how far south the front (or convective outflows) track \nbefore stalling and weakening. This will ultimately determine our \nprecip chances Wed evening into Thurs. By this point in the \nforecast, the center of the upper ridge will be shifting to the SW\nCONUS, resulting in NW flow aloft locally that advects some of \nthe monsoonal moisture and results in height falls aloft, which \nalone isn't sufficient for precip but at least makes conditions a \nbit more supportive compared to being directly beneath the ridge.\n\nAs has been the case in previous forecast cycles, guidance still\nfavors the front staying north of our area Wed PM, keeping PoPs \nhigher across WFO Amarillo's FA compared to ours. However, the \nfew high-res models that do go out that far depict an outflow \npushing across the northern half of our FA, so perhaps that could \nkick off some convection. The deep well-mixed boundary layer (with\nDCAPE values between 1000-1500 J/kg) will certainly support \nprogressive cold pools/outflows. \n\nWhether it is the front itself sagging south or remnant outflows \naiding precip development, the model blend has the highest PoPs on\nThurs (20-40%). While a dramatic cooldown is unlikely, guidance \ndoes favor temps a few degrees cooler than Tues-Wed, with highs in\nthe mid 90s on the Caprock and upper 90s off the Caprock. Temps \nquickly rebound late week into the weekend as the upper ridge \nshifts back east, albeit with heights not as strong as our mid-\nweek ridge. Nevertheless, 500mb heights are expected to be near or\nabove the 90th percentile of the model climatology, resulting in \nhighs back in the upper 90s (on the Caprock) to low 100s (off the \nCaprock). These forecast values are a few degrees shy of the \nrecords for Sep 11-12. \n\nGuidance is slowly coming into better agreement regarding the\ntiming of the next Pacific NW upper low, favoring its emergence \nonto the northern Plains early next week. This may lead to a \nwelcome cooling trend next week (although broad model spread \nremains regarding the exact extent of the cooling). Both the GEFS \nand EC Ens have a reasonable chance (20-50%) of below normal temps\nnext Tues-Wed (Sep 15-16). Normal highs for mid-September are in \nthe mid-80s. Some skepticism of this cool down is still warranted,\nhowever, as deterministic models depict the anomalous upper ridge\npersisting across portions of the south-central or southeast US \nnext week.",
